1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
You can't code against a goal.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
You have to turn it into a functional requirement document

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
that a developer can build and QA verifies.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This lesson covers how to trace a business need

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
down to a concrete testable spec. Let's dive in.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Let's anchor three terms

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
because the whole lesson turns on keeping them straight.

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
First, a business need.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
That's the outcome a stakeholder actually wants,

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
something like cutting ticket triage time in half.

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
It's real value, but you can't code against it

12
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
because it names no behavior.

13
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Second, a functional requirement.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
That's a specific behavior your application delivers,

15
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
written as an input and the output you expect back.

16
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Third, acceptance criteria.

17
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Those are the concrete conditions

18
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
that prove the requirement is met, written,

19
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
so a developer can code straight to them

20
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
and QA can verify each one.

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Need, requirement, criteria.

22
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,000
You move left to right

23
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
and each step gets more testable than the last.

24
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Here's the discipline that keeps a project honest.

25
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Every functional requirement

26
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
should trace back to a business need.

27
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,000
So start at the outcome.

28
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Write down in plain language

29
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
what the business is actually trying to achieve.

30
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Then break that outcome into user-observable capabilities,

31
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
the things a user would see the app do.

32
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
For a triage goal, that might be classify the ticket,

33
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
route it, and draft a reply.

34
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Only then do you map each capability

35
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
to a single clawed interaction that delivers it.

36
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
When you work outcome first,

37
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
capability second, interaction last,

38
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
your scope stays tied to real value

39
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
and you avoid building clever features nobody asked for.

40
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
This distinction trips up a lot of specs,

41
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
so let's separate the two.

42
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Business intent is how the product should feel,

43
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
fast, trustworthy, effortless.

44
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
That matters, but notice it doesn't yet name a behavior

45
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
you can build or test.

46
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Your job is to translate it into one.

47
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Functional behavior is what the application actually does

48
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
with a given input.

49
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
It returns a valid JSON object

50
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
holding the caller's name, email, and plan.

51
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,000
See the difference?

52
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
One is a feeling, the other is a contract.

53
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Your job as a developer is to hear the intent,

54
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
respect it, and then translate it into concrete behavior.

55
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Feelings guide the design.

56
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Behaviors are what you actually write code against and hand to QA.

57
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Let's get practical about decomposition.

58
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
A big business outcome almost always hides

59
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
several smaller capabilities inside it,

60
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
and your job is to pull them out.

61
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Walk through the user's experience

62
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
and list each thing they would actually watch the app do.

63
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Keep every item observable,

64
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,000
something a person could point at,

65
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
not internal plumbing like which model you called.

66
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And size each capability so it maps cleanly

67
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
to a single interaction with Claude.

68
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
If a capability feels too big to test in one shot,

69
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,000
split it further.

70
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
When you finish, you should have a short list

71
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
of small, visible capabilities,

72
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
and each one becomes a testable requirement

73
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
you can verify on its own.

74
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Once you have a capability, you pick how Claude delivers it,

75
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
and there are three tiers to choose from.

76
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
The first is a single call.

77
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
One request in, one response out.

78
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
That covers a huge amount of real work,

79
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
classification, summarization, extraction,

80
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:32,000
a direct question.

81
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
The second is a workflow.

82
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
That's a fixed sequence of steps you author in code

83
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
where you decide the path at design time.

84
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
The third is an agent.

85
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
That's an open-ended loop where the model itself

86
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
chooses each next action at runtime

87
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
until the task is finished.

88
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Single call, workflow, agent.

89
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
They climb in power,

90
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
but they also climb in cost and complexity,

91
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
which sets up the rule coming next.

92
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Now, the rule that saves you the most grief.

93
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Always reach for the simplest tier

94
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
that genuinely meets the need.

95
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Developers love reaching straight for an agent

96
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
because agents feel powerful and modern,

97
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
but an agent isn't your default.

98
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Reach for it only when the four checks coming up say so.

99
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Simpler tiers are cheaper to run,

100
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
faster to respond, and far easier to test

101
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
because their behavior is predictable.

102
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
So start at the bottom.

103
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Ask whether a single call gets the job done.

104
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,000
If it can't, ask whether a fixed workflow can.

105
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Only climb to an agent

106
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
when the simpler options genuinely fall short.

107
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Start low and let the requirement, not your enthusiasm,

108
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
be the thing that pushes you higher.

109
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
If you think a capability really does need an agent,

110
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
run it through four honest checks before you commit.

111
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
First, complexity.

112
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Is the task genuinely multi-step

113
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
and hard to script in advance,

114
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
or could a fixed workflow handle it? Second, value.

115
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Does the outcome justify an agent's higher cost

116
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
and slower response?

117
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Third, viability.

118
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Is Claude actually capable

119
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
of the open-ended task you're imagining?

120
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And fourth, cost of error.

121
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
If the agent takes a wrong step,

122
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
is that recoverable or is it catastrophic?

123
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
If any one of those four comes back weak,

124
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
the honest answer is to meet the requirement

125
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,000
at a simpler tier.

126
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Passing all four is what earns the agent.

127
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Here's a failure mode worth naming

128
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
because it hides in plain sight.

129
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Sometimes what looks like a functional behavior

130
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,000
is really an infrastructure constraint, wearing a disguise.

131
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
A stakeholder says the app feels slow,

132
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
so you dutifully write a requirement

133
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
to make Claude answer faster.

134
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
That reads like functional behavior, but it isn't.

135
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
The real driver is a latency budget,

136
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
or a hosting region, or a throughput ceiling.

137
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
None of those get fixed by rewording a prompt.

138
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,000
They get fixed in the architecture,

139
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
and they belong in infrastructure requirements,

140
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
which is a separate lesson.

141
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
So when a requirement smells like performance,

142
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
cost, or capacity, stop and ask

143
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
whether you're describing a behavior at all,

144
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
or an operating constraint in disguise. Let's recap.

145
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
You turn a business need into a testable spec

146
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
by decomposing the outcome, telling behavior from intent,

147
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
and picking the simplest tier.

148
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Keep in mind, some behaviors

149
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
are really infrastructure in disguise.

150
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
That covers turning a business need into a testable spec.

151
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Come with me to the next video.

